Java is an **object-oriented** language. A **class** is a blueprint; an **object** is an instance of it.

Define a class with fields (data) and methods (behavior):

```java
class Player {
    String name;
    int hp;

    void takeDamage(int amount) {
        hp = hp - amount;
    }
}
```

Create and use an object with `new`:

```java
Player hero = new Player();
hero.name = "Aria";
hero.hp = 100;
hero.takeDamage(25);
System.out.println(hero.hp);   // 75
```

A **constructor** sets up the object on creation — same name as the class:

```java
class Player {
    String name;
    int hp;

    Player(String n, int h) {
        name = n;
        hp = h;
    }
}

Player hero = new Player("Aria", 100);
```

Encapsulation: keep fields `private` and expose them through `public` **getters/setters** so you control how they're read and changed:

```java
class Player {
    private int hp;

    public int getHp() {
        return hp;
    }

    public void setHp(int value) {
        hp = value;
    }
}
```

